window.BioChecks[34] = {"2004-Q12c":[{"q":"Describe the nervous and hormonal responses when body temperature drops. (3 points)","right":"Brain receptors detect it; muscles shiver; thyroxine raises metabolism","wrong":["Sweat glands release sweat; skin vessels widen; thyroxine falls","Insulin is released; the heart slows; sweating starts","Muscles relax; skin vessels widen; adrenaline stops"],"why":"Receptors in the skin and brain detect the drop; nerves make the muscles shiver to generate heat; thyroxine raises the metabolic rate.","hint":"Three stages: what detects the change, what the muscles do, and what a hormone does to metabolism.","board":true,"hook":"Cold? Detect it, shiver, and turn up the thyroxine furnace","keys":["receptors in the skin and brain","shiver","thyroxine raises the metabolic rate"]}],"2005-Q3c":[{"q":"True or false: endocrine glands secrete hormones.","right":"True","wrong":["False"],"why":"Endocrine glands are ductless glands that secrete hormones straight into the blood.","hint":"Think of what the pituitary and thyroid make, and where it goes.","board":true,"hook":"ENDOcrine: hormones go IN to the blood, no duct needed","keys":["ductless","secrete hormones"]}],"2005-Q13a":[{"q":"Where is testosterone secreted in the male body?","right":"Testes","wrong":["Pituitary gland","Adrenal glands","Prostate gland"],"why":"Testosterone is made in the testes, which hang outside the body in the scrotum.","hint":"It's made in the male sex organs that also make sperm.","board":true,"hook":"TESTosterone from the TESTes: the name gives the factory away","keys":["testes","scrotum"]},{"q":"Give a brief account of the role of testosterone. (2 points)","right":"Sperm production and male secondary sexual characteristics","wrong":["Making eggs and thickening the uterus lining","Raising the heart rate and blood glucose","Controlling the metabolic rate and calcium"],"why":"Testosterone starts sperm production, develops the male sex organs and gives secondary sexual characteristics: deeper voice, facial hair, more muscle.","hint":"Think of what changes in boys at puberty, and what the testes start making.","board":true,"hook":"Testosterone: sperm, sex organs and the puberty package (voice, beard, muscle)","keys":["sperm production","secondary sexual characteristics"]}],"2005-Q14c":[{"q":"The pancreas is an exocrine and an endocrine gland. Explain both terms. (2 points)","right":"Exocrine: has ducts; endocrine: ductless, secretes into the blood","wrong":["Exocrine: secretes into the blood; endocrine: into ducts","Exocrine: makes hormones; endocrine: makes enzymes","Exocrine: inside the gut; endocrine: inside the brain"],"why":"Exocrine glands release their products through ducts; endocrine glands are ductless and secrete hormones straight into the blood.","hint":"One kind of gland empties down a tube; the other has none and uses the bloodstream.","board":true,"hook":"EXOcrine EXits through a duct. ENDOcrine ENters the blood","keys":["through ducts","ductless","straight into the blood"]},{"q":"Name a product of the endocrine pancreas and give one of its functions. (2 points)","right":"Insulin: it lowers blood glucose","wrong":["Amylase: it digests starch","Adrenaline: it raises the heart rate","Thyroxine: it controls metabolism"],"why":"The islets of Langerhans secrete insulin into the blood; it makes cells take in glucose, lowering blood glucose.","hint":"The islets of Langerhans make the hormone diabetics inject. What does it do to blood sugar?","board":true,"hook":"INsulin puts glucose INto cells, so blood glucose goes down","keys":["insulin","lowering blood glucose"]}],"2007-Q4":[{"q":"Where in the body is FSH produced?","right":"Pituitary gland","wrong":["Ovaries","Uterus","Thyroid gland"],"why":"FSH (follicle stimulating hormone) is made in the pituitary gland at the base of the brain and travels in the blood to the ovaries.","hint":"It's a 'master gland' hormone: made at the base of the brain, acting on the ovaries.","board":true,"hook":"Pituitary: the master gland that bosses the ovaries with FSH and LH","keys":["pituitary gland","base of the brain"]}],"2007-Q15a":[{"q":"State two functions of testosterone. (2 points)","right":"Sperm production and the growth of facial hair","wrong":["Egg production and the development of breasts","Ovulation and the repair of the uterus lining","Raising blood glucose and the heart rate"],"why":"Testosterone causes sperm production and the male secondary sexual characteristics, such as facial hair, a deeper voice and more muscle.","hint":"One job is in the testes themselves; the other shows on the outside at puberty.","board":true,"hook":"Testosterone makes sperm and men: beard, deep voice, muscle","keys":["sperm production","facial hair"]}],"2007-Q15b":[{"q":"Apart from secreting hormones, how does an endocrine gland differ from an exocrine gland?","right":"It has no duct: it secretes into the blood","wrong":["It has ducts that carry its products away","It makes enzymes, not hormones","It is controlled only by nerves"],"why":"Endocrine glands are ductless: their secretions pass straight into the bloodstream. Exocrine glands empty through ducts.","hint":"Think plumbing: does it have a tube to carry its product away, or not?","board":true,"hook":"No duct, so the blood is the delivery van","keys":["ductless","straight into the bloodstream"]},{"q":"State two ways in which hormone action differs from nerve action. (2 points)","right":"Hormones are slower and their effects last longer","wrong":["Hormones are faster and their effects are shorter","Hormones are electrical and act on one muscle only","Hormones travel along neurons and act at once"],"why":"Hormones are chemicals carried in the blood, so they act more slowly, their effects last longer, and they can reach many target organs.","hint":"Compare a text message with a letter in the post: speed, how long it lasts, how many it reaches.","board":true,"hook":"Nerves: a phone call, fast and brief. Hormones: a letter, slow but lasting","keys":["more slowly","last longer","many target organs"]},{"q":"Give the thyroid gland's location, hormone and the hormone's role. (one of 8 points)","right":"In the neck; thyroxine; controls the metabolic rate","wrong":["At the base of the brain; FSH; ovulation","Behind the stomach; glucagon; digestion","Beside the uterus; oestrogen; the menstrual cycle"],"why":"The thyroid is in the neck, in front of the trachea; it secretes thyroxine, which controls the metabolic rate (and growth in the young).","hint":"The gland sits in your neck. Its hormone sets how fast your cells use energy.","board":true,"hook":"Thyroid, throat, thyroxine: three Ts that set your metabolic rate","keys":["in the neck","thyroxine","metabolic rate"]},{"q":"Name a hormone and a corrective measure for its deficiency. (2 points)","right":"Insulin: given by injection","wrong":["Glycogen: given by injection","Amylase: taken as tablets","Haemoglobin: a blood transfusion"],"why":"The corrective measure for a lacking hormone is to supply it: insulin injections for diabetes, or thyroxine tablets.","hint":"If the body can't make enough of a hormone, how could a doctor top it up?","board":true,"hook":"Missing a hormone? Top it up: insulin injections, thyroxine tablets","keys":["insulin injections","thyroxine tablets"]}],"2008-Q13c":[{"q":"Blood water content drops. Name the hormone released and its endocrine gland. (2 points)","right":"ADH, from the pituitary gland","wrong":["ADH, from the adrenal gland","Insulin, from the pancreas","Thyroxine, from the thyroid gland"],"why":"The brain detects the low water level; the pituitary releases ADH, so the kidneys reabsorb more water and make less urine.","hint":"The hormone is 'anti-diuretic': it saves water. It comes from the pea-sized gland under the brain.","board":true,"hook":"ADH: Anti-Diuretic, against weeing. Short of water? The pituitary sends it","keys":["pituitary releases ADH","reabsorb more water"]},{"q":"Give a precise target area for ADH, and say how ADH reaches it. (2 points)","right":"The collecting ducts of the kidney, carried in the blood","wrong":["The Bowman's capsule, carried along nerves","The glomerulus, carried in the lymph","The bladder, carried in the urine"],"why":"ADH acts on the distal tubules and collecting ducts of the nephrons; like every hormone it is carried there in the blood.","hint":"Its target is near the end of the nephron. How does any hormone travel round the body?","board":true,"hook":"ADH opens the water gates at the end of the nephron; the blood delivers it","keys":["collecting ducts","in the blood"]}],"2010-Q11c":[{"q":"What term describes the glands that secrete hormones?","right":"Endocrine glands","wrong":["Exocrine glands","Salivary glands","Lymph glands"],"why":"Endocrine glands are ductless glands that secrete hormones directly into the blood.","hint":"The name means 'secreting within': these glands have no ducts.","board":true,"hook":"ENDOcrine: ENDs up IN the blood","keys":["ductless","directly into the blood"]},{"q":"Name a hormone-producing gland, where it is, a hormone it makes and its role. (4 points)","right":"Pancreas, behind the stomach; insulin lowers blood glucose","wrong":["Thyroid, in the neck; thyroxine lowers blood glucose","Pituitary, in the neck; ADH lowers blood glucose","Pancreas, in the brain; glucagon lowers blood glucose"],"why":"The pancreas lies behind the stomach; its islets of Langerhans secrete insulin, which lowers blood glucose by making cells take it in.","hint":"Pick one gland you know well and give four facts: name, place, hormone, job.","board":true,"hook":"Gland, place, hormone, job: pancreas, behind the stomach, insulin, glucose down","keys":["behind the stomach","insulin","lowers blood glucose"]},{"q":"Give two examples of the use of hormone supplements. (2 points)","right":"Insulin for diabetes, and the contraceptive pill","wrong":["Antibiotics for infections, and vaccines for flu","Vitamin C for scurvy, and iron for anaemia","Painkillers for headaches, and statins"],"why":"Hormones given as medicines: insulin injections treat diabetes, and the contraceptive pill (oestrogen and progesterone) prevents pregnancy.","hint":"One is injected by people whose pancreas fails; one is taken daily to prevent pregnancy.","board":true,"hook":"Hormone supplements: insulin for diabetics, the pill for contraception","keys":["insulin injections treat diabetes","contraceptive pill"]}],"2010-Q15a":[{"q":"What maintains the secondary sexual characteristics in the adult male?","right":"Testosterone","wrong":["Oestrogen","Progesterone","FSH"],"why":"Testosterone from the testes keeps the male secondary sexual characteristics, such as facial hair and a deep voice, throughout adult life.","hint":"It's the main male sex hormone, made in the testes.","board":true,"hook":"Testosterone keeps the beard growing and the voice deep for life","keys":["testosterone","throughout adult life"]}],"2011-Q11c":[{"q":"What is a hormone?","right":"A chemical messenger made by an endocrine gland","wrong":["A protein that speeds up a reaction","An electrical impulse carried by nerves","A substance made by an exocrine gland"],"why":"A hormone is a chemical messenger, secreted by an endocrine gland into the blood, that acts on a target organ.","hint":"Think of it as a message, but made of chemicals and sent through the blood.","board":true,"hook":"Hormone = a chemical text message sent through the bloodstream","keys":["chemical messenger","endocrine gland","target organ"]},{"q":"State two ways in which hormones are similar to plant growth regulators. (2 points)","right":"Both are made in one place and act in a different place","wrong":["Both are carried in the blood and made of protein","Both act instantly and only for a short time","Both are enzymes that speed up reactions"],"why":"Hormones and plant growth regulators are both made in one place, act in a different place, and have prolonged effects.","hint":"Think where each is made compared with where it works, and how long its effect lasts.","board":true,"hook":"Animal or plant: made here, works over there, and keeps working","keys":["made in one place","act in a different place","prolonged effects"]},{"q":"What is meant by feedback in hormone action? Give an example. (4 points)","right":"The level of a hormone controls another: high thyroxine inhibits TSH","wrong":["The level of an enzyme controls another: high amylase stops lipase","Nerves control a gland: impulses make the thyroid stop","A hormone's level never changes: thyroxine is made steadily"],"why":"Feedback: the level of a hormone in the blood controls the production of another or itself; high thyroxine inhibits TSH from the pituitary.","hint":"One hormone's level acts as a signal back to the gland that made the hormone controlling it.","board":true,"hook":"Feedback is a thermostat: enough thyroxine switches off the TSH heater","keys":["level of a hormone","high thyroxine inhibits TSH"]},{"q":"Name a hormone and a condition caused by too little of it.","right":"Insulin: diabetes","wrong":["Insulin: scurvy","Thyroxine: diabetes","Adrenaline: anaemia"],"why":"With too little insulin, blood glucose is not brought down: this is diabetes, which is treated with insulin injections.","hint":"Think of the condition people manage with insulin injections.","board":false,"hook":"No insulin, no glucose control: that's diabetes","keys":["too little insulin","diabetes"]}],"2012-Q13a":[{"q":"Give one way a nervous response differs from a hormonal response.","right":"Nervous responses are faster","wrong":["Nervous responses last longer","Nervous responses are chemical only","Nervous responses reach more organs"],"why":"Nerve impulses are electrical, so a nervous response is faster and shorter-lived than a hormonal one.","hint":"Compare pulling your hand off a hot pan with growing up at puberty.","board":true,"hook":"Nerves: lightning. Hormones: the slow tide","keys":["electrical","faster","shorter-lived"]}],"2013-Q1d":[{"q":"Distinguish between enzymes and hormones. (2 points)","right":"An enzyme is a biological catalyst; a hormone is a chemical messenger","wrong":["An enzyme is an electrical signal; a hormone is a type of fat","Enzymes are made by nerves; hormones are made in the gut","Enzymes are only in the stomach; hormones are only in plants"],"why":"Enzymes are biological catalysts that speed up reactions; hormones are chemical messengers carried in the blood to a target organ.","hint":"One speeds up reactions; the other carries a message. Which is which?","board":true,"hook":"Enzymes do the work, hormones send the orders","keys":["biological catalysts","chemical messengers"]}],"2013-Q10a":[{"q":"What term is used for glands that secrete hormones?","right":"Endocrine","wrong":["Exocrine","Lymphatic","Mammary"],"why":"Glands that secrete hormones are endocrine glands: they have no ducts and release hormones into the blood.","hint":"The opposite of glands that empty through ducts.","board":true,"hook":"ENDO = within: endocrine glands release hormones within the blood","keys":["endocrine glands","no ducts"]},{"q":"How do hormone-secreting glands differ from glands that do not secrete hormones?","right":"They have no ducts","wrong":["They have ducts","They are bigger","They are controlled by nerves"],"why":"Endocrine glands are ductless: they secrete straight into the blood, while exocrine glands empty through ducts.","hint":"Exocrine glands have a tube to carry their product away. What do endocrine glands lack?","board":true,"hook":"Endocrine: no pipes, the blood carries it","keys":["ductless","through ducts"]},{"q":"Explain why the pancreas is called a dual-function gland.","right":"It makes hormones (insulin) and digestive enzymes","wrong":["It makes both bile and saliva","It is in both the chest and the abdomen","It digests both fats and proteins"],"why":"The pancreas is endocrine and exocrine: its islets secrete insulin into the blood, and other cells make digestive enzymes that flow down a duct.","hint":"It has two products: one goes into the blood, one goes down a duct to the gut.","board":true,"hook":"Pancreas: two jobs, insulin into the blood and enzymes down the duct","keys":["endocrine and exocrine","insulin into the blood","digestive enzymes"]}],"2013-Q10b":[{"q":"What is the chemical nature of many hormones?","right":"Protein","wrong":["Carbohydrate","Nucleic acid","Mineral salt"],"why":"Many hormones, such as insulin, are proteins: chains of amino acids made on the ribosomes of gland cells.","hint":"Insulin is one: it's made of amino acids joined in chains.","board":true,"hook":"Insulin is a protein: many hormones are amino acid chains","keys":["proteins","insulin"]},{"q":"For two named hormones, give their gland's location and a function. (6 points)","right":"Thyroxine: neck, metabolism; insulin: behind stomach, lowers glucose","wrong":["Thyroxine: brain, lowers glucose; insulin: neck, metabolism","Adrenaline: neck, growth; insulin: brain, digestion","ADH: stomach, metabolism; insulin: kidney, growth"],"why":"Thyroxine: the thyroid in the neck, controls the metabolic rate. Insulin: the pancreas behind the stomach, lowers blood glucose.","hint":"Choose two you know well, such as the thyroid's hormone and the pancreas's.","board":true,"hook":"Neck sets the pace (thyroxine); behind the stomach sets the sugar (insulin)","keys":["in the neck","metabolic rate","lowers blood glucose"]},{"q":"How is a deficiency of thyroxine corrected?","right":"Taking thyroxine tablets","wrong":["Removing the thyroid gland","Insulin injections","Eating less sugar"],"why":"A gland that makes too little hormone is corrected by supplying it: thyroxine tablets, or insulin injections for diabetes.","hint":"If the gland can't make enough, where else could the hormone come from?","board":true,"hook":"Not enough thyroxine? Take it as a tablet","keys":["thyroxine tablets","supplying it"]},{"q":"Explain why hormonal responses are slower than nervous responses. (2 points)","right":"Hormones travel in the blood; nerve impulses are electrical","wrong":["Hormones travel along nerves; nerve impulses are chemical","Hormones must be digested first; nerves skip the brain","Hormones are made slowly; nerve impulses travel in lymph"],"why":"Hormones are chemicals that travel in the blood to their targets; nerve impulses are electrical and travel along neurons.","hint":"How does each signal travel: through which system, and in what form?","board":true,"hook":"Blood is a slow river; a nerve is a live wire","keys":["travel in the blood","electrical"]}],"2013-Q13a":[{"q":"Which hormone maintains a male's secondary sexual characteristics throughout life?","right":"Testosterone","wrong":["Oestrogen","LH","Thyroxine"],"why":"Testosterone from the testes maintains the male secondary sexual characteristics, such as a deep voice and facial hair, for life.","hint":"The male sex hormone; oestrogen does this job in females.","board":true,"hook":"Oestrogen shapes her, testosterone shapes him, for life","keys":["testosterone","for life"]}],"2016-Q15b":[{"q":"Where in the body are FSH and LH produced?","right":"Pituitary gland","wrong":["Ovaries","Uterus","Adrenal glands"],"why":"FSH and LH are made in the pituitary gland at the base of the brain; they travel in the blood to the ovaries.","hint":"Neither is made in the ovary: both come from the master gland under the brain.","board":true,"hook":"FSH and LH: the pituitary's two messages to the ovaries","keys":["pituitary gland","to the ovaries"]}],"2016-Q15c":[{"q":"Name a hormone and one sign that too much of it is being secreted.","right":"Thyroxine: a high metabolic rate","wrong":["Insulin: high blood glucose","ADH: lots of dilute urine","Thyroxine: feeling cold and sluggish"],"why":"Thyroxine controls the metabolic rate, so too much of it is recognised by a metabolic rate raised above normal.","hint":"Think what thyroxine normally controls, then imagine it turned up too high.","board":false,"hook":"Thyroxine sets the pace; too much and everything runs too fast","keys":["controls the metabolic rate","too much"]}],"2018-Q6":[{"q":"Name the endocrine gland that produces ADH.","right":"Pituitary gland","wrong":["Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pancreas"],"why":"The scheme gives the hypothalamus and allows the pituitary; on the new course ADH is a hormone of the pituitary, at the base of the brain.","hint":"ADH comes from the pea-sized gland that hangs below the brain.","board":true,"hook":"ADH: the pituitary's water-saving message to the kidneys","keys":["pituitary","base of the brain"]}],"2018-Q14c":[{"q":"The pancreas is both exocrine and endocrine. Define the term endocrine.","right":"Ductless: it secretes its products straight into the blood","wrong":["It secretes its products into a duct","It makes digestive enzymes","It responds to nerve impulses"],"why":"Endocrine means ductless: the gland secretes its products, hormones, directly into the bloodstream.","hint":"'Endo' means within. Where do its products go, and is there a tube?","board":true,"hook":"Endocrine: no duct, straight into the blood","keys":["ductless","directly into the bloodstream"]},{"q":"For the endocrine pancreas, give a substance, its tissue, a site of action and its role. (4 points)","right":"Insulin; islets of Langerhans; the liver; lowers blood glucose","wrong":["Amylase; islets of Langerhans; the gut; digests starch","Insulin; the thyroid; the brain; raises blood glucose","Glucagon; the pancreatic duct; the gut; digests fat"],"why":"The islets of Langerhans secrete insulin; it acts on body cells and the liver, which take in glucose (stored as glycogen), lowering blood glucose.","hint":"Start with the hormone diabetics inject, then where in the pancreas it's made.","board":true,"hook":"Islets make insulin; liver and muscle soak up the glucose","keys":["islets of Langerhans","insulin","lowering blood glucose"]},{"q":"Describe how a feedback mechanism works in the human endocrine system. (3 points)","right":"Thyroxine secretion inhibits secretion of another hormone, TSH","wrong":["Thyroxine secretion makes the thyroid grow bigger","Nerve impulses switch the thyroid off when it is tired","TSH is broken down by enzymes in the liver"],"why":"Secretion of one hormone inhibits (negative) or stimulates (positive) secretion of another or itself: high thyroxine inhibits TSH, so less thyroxine is made.","hint":"Use thyroxine and TSH: what does a high level of thyroxine do to the pituitary?","board":true,"hook":"Thyroxine tells the pituitary 'enough': less TSH, less thyroxine","keys":["inhibits","another or itself","high thyroxine inhibits TSH"]},{"q":"Give two examples of the use of hormone supplements. (2 points)","right":"Fertility treatment and HRT for the menopause","wrong":["Antibiotics for infection and vaccines for measles","Painkillers and antihistamines","Iron tablets and vitamin D drops"],"why":"Hormone supplements include insulin to treat diabetes, the contraceptive pill, fertility treatment (FSH) and HRT (oestrogen) at the menopause.","hint":"Think of hormones given to help people have a baby, or women through the menopause.","board":true,"hook":"Supplements: insulin, the pill, fertility drugs, HRT","keys":["fertility treatment","HRT"]}],"2019-Q14a":[{"q":"Endocrine glands produce hormones. What is meant by the term endocrine?","right":"Ductless: secreting straight into the blood","wrong":["Secreting through a duct","Making digestive enzymes","Carrying electrical impulses"],"why":"Endocrine means ductless: an endocrine gland secretes its hormones directly into the blood.","hint":"Compare it with exocrine glands, which empty through tubes.","board":true,"hook":"ENDOcrine: secretes INto the blood, no duct","keys":["ductless","directly into the blood"]},{"q":"Name a hormone secreted by the pituitary gland and give its function. (2 points)","right":"TSH: it stimulates the thyroid to make thyroxine","wrong":["Insulin: it lowers blood glucose","Thyroxine: it controls the metabolic rate","Adrenaline: it raises the heart rate"],"why":"The pituitary makes FSH, LH, TSH, ADH and oxytocin; TSH stimulates the thyroid to secrete thyroxine.","hint":"Its name says what it does: it stimulates another gland, in the neck.","board":true,"hook":"TSH = Thyroid Stimulating Hormone: the name is the job","keys":["TSH stimulates the thyroid","FSH, LH, TSH, ADH and oxytocin"]},{"q":"Name one gland that has both an endocrine and an exocrine function.","right":"Pancreas","wrong":["Thyroid gland","Pituitary gland","Adrenal gland"],"why":"The pancreas secretes insulin into the blood (endocrine) and digestive enzymes down a duct (exocrine).","hint":"It makes a hormone for blood sugar and enzymes for the duodenum.","board":true,"hook":"Pancreas: one gland, two delivery routes","keys":["insulin into the blood","digestive enzymes"]}],"2020-Q14a":[{"q":"Where is the hormone oestrogen produced?","right":"Ovaries","wrong":["Pituitary gland","Uterus","Adrenal glands"],"why":"Oestrogen is made in the ovaries (by the developing Graafian follicle) and acts on the uterus lining.","hint":"It's made in the female sex organs, which also make the eggs.","board":true,"hook":"Ovaries make eggs and oestrogen","keys":["ovaries","Graafian follicle"]}],"2021-Q6a":[{"q":"True or false: adrenaline is secreted into the blood when you face a threat.","right":"True","wrong":["False"],"why":"Adrenaline from the adrenal glands prepares the body for 'fight or flight': faster heart rate and breathing, more glucose in the blood.","hint":"Think of the hormone behind a racing heart when you get a fright.","board":true,"hook":"Fright, fight or flight: adrenaline floods the blood","keys":["fight or flight","faster heart rate"]}],"2021-Q16b":[{"q":"Name the hormone responsible for male secondary sexual characteristics.","right":"Testosterone","wrong":["Oestrogen","Progesterone","Adrenaline"],"why":"Testosterone, made in the testes, causes the male secondary sexual characteristics at puberty: facial hair, a deeper voice, more muscle.","hint":"The male sex hormone, made from puberty on.","board":true,"hook":"Puberty in boys runs on testosterone","keys":["testosterone","at puberty"]}],"2021-Q17c":[{"q":"Give two ways in which a nervous response differs from a hormonal response. (2 points)","right":"Nervous: fast and electrical; hormonal: slow and chemical","wrong":["Nervous: travels in the blood; hormonal: travels along nerves","Nervous: affects many organs; hormonal: affects one muscle","Nervous: lasts for years; hormonal: lasts a second"],"why":"Nervous responses are fast, electrical and short-lived; hormonal responses are slower, chemical (in the blood) and longer-lasting.","hint":"Think speed, the kind of signal, and how long the effect lasts.","board":true,"hook":"Nerves: fast, electric, brief. Hormones: slow, chemical, lasting","keys":["fast, electrical and short-lived","slower, chemical"]}],"2023-Q17a":[{"q":"Give one difference between endocrine and exocrine glands.","right":"Endocrine glands have no ducts; exocrine glands do","wrong":["Exocrine glands make hormones; endocrine glands make enzymes","Endocrine glands are controlled only by nerves","Exocrine glands are found only in the brain"],"why":"Endocrine glands are ductless and secrete into the blood; exocrine glands secrete through ducts, e.g. salivary glands.","hint":"Think of a tube: which kind of gland has one to carry its product away?","board":true,"hook":"Exocrine Exits by a duct; endocrine Enters the blood","keys":["ductless","secrete through ducts"]},{"q":"Name a gland with endocrine and exocrine functions, and each product. (3 points)","right":"Pancreas: insulin (endocrine) and digestive enzymes (exocrine)","wrong":["Thyroid: thyroxine (endocrine) and saliva (exocrine)","Pancreas: bile (endocrine) and insulin (exocrine)","Pituitary: ADH (endocrine) and sweat (exocrine)"],"why":"The pancreas is both: its islets secrete insulin into the blood, and its other cells secrete digestive enzymes (pancreatic juice) down a duct.","hint":"One gland makes a blood-glucose hormone and a digestive juice for the duodenum.","board":true,"hook":"Pancreas: insulin to the blood, enzymes to the gut","keys":["insulin","digestive enzymes","down a duct"]},{"q":"Where are the pituitary, thyroid and adrenal glands? (3 points)","right":"Base of the brain; neck; on top of each kidney","wrong":["Neck; base of the brain; on top of each kidney","Base of the brain; behind the stomach; neck","Chest; neck; beside the uterus"],"why":"The pituitary hangs from the base of the brain, the thyroid sits in the neck in front of the trachea, and an adrenal gland sits on top of each kidney.","hint":"Work down the body: head, then neck, then the organs that make urine.","board":true,"hook":"Head, neck, kidneys: pituitary, thyroid, adrenals, top to bottom","keys":["base of the brain","in the neck","on top of each kidney"]},{"q":"Name a hormone of the thyroid and of the adrenals, and give each one's function. (4 points)","right":"Thyroxine controls metabolism; adrenaline, fight or flight","wrong":["Thyroxine lowers blood glucose; adrenaline, fight or flight","Insulin controls metabolism; adrenaline, fight or flight","Thyroxine controls metabolism; ADH, fight or flight"],"why":"The thyroid secretes thyroxine, which controls the metabolic rate; the adrenals secrete adrenaline, which prepares the body for fight or flight.","hint":"One hormone sets the body's pace every day; the other handles emergencies.","board":true,"hook":"Thyroxine: the everyday pace. Adrenaline: the emergency button","keys":["thyroxine","metabolic rate","fight or flight"]}],"2024-Q16b":[{"q":"On the outline of a female body, name glands X, Y and Z. (3 points)","right":"Thyroid, adrenal glands, ovaries","wrong":["Thyroid, pancreas, ovaries","Pituitary, adrenal glands, ovaries","Thyroid, adrenal glands, uterus"],"why":"X in the neck is the thyroid, Y on top of each kidney is an adrenal gland, and Z either side of the uterus is an ovary.","hint":"Neck, top of the kidneys, either side of the uterus: one gland at each.","board":"Name the glands in the neck, on top of each kidney, and either side of the uterus. (3 points)","hook":"Neck, kidneys, pelvis: thyroid, adrenals, ovaries","keys":["thyroid","adrenal gland","ovary"]},{"q":"Name a hormone of the thyroid, adrenals and ovaries, and a function of each. (6 points)","right":"Thyroxine: metabolism; adrenaline: fight or flight; oestrogen: breasts","wrong":["Thyroxine: fight or flight; adrenaline: metabolism; oestrogen: breasts","Insulin: metabolism; adrenaline: fight or flight; oestrogen: breasts","Thyroxine: metabolism; ADH: fight or flight; FSH: breasts"],"why":"Thyroxine controls the metabolic rate; adrenaline prepares the body for fight or flight; oestrogen causes female secondary sexual characteristics.","hint":"Thyroid: the body's pace. Adrenals: emergencies. Ovaries: female development.","board":true,"hook":"Thyroid paces, adrenals panic, ovaries shape","keys":["metabolic rate","fight or flight","secondary sexual characteristics"]},{"q":"For a named hormone, give a corrective measure for its deficiency.","right":"Insulin: injections or an insulin pump","wrong":["Insulin: removing the pancreas","Thyroxine: removing the thyroid","Insulin: eating more sugar"],"why":"A hormone deficiency is corrected by giving the hormone: people with diabetes inject insulin or use an insulin pump.","hint":"If the pancreas can't make enough insulin, how does the body get it?","board":true,"hook":"Diabetes: the insulin comes from a pen or a pump, not the pancreas","keys":["giving the hormone","insulin pump"]},{"q":"Briefly describe the feedback mechanism of one human hormone. (2 points)","right":"When thyroxine secretion is high it inhibits TSH secretion","wrong":["TSH is made by the thyroid and broken down in the liver","Nerve impulses switch the thyroid off when it is tired","Thyroxine is stored in the pancreas until it is needed"],"why":"The level of one hormone controls the secretion of another or itself: a high level of thyroxine inhibits TSH secretion by the pituitary.","hint":"Thyroxine and TSH: what happens to TSH when there's plenty of thyroxine?","board":true,"hook":"Plenty of thyroxine? The pituitary eases off the TSH","keys":["level of one hormone","inhibits TSH secretion"]}],"2025-Q17c":[{"q":"ADH is an important hormone in homeostasis. Name the endocrine gland that secretes it.","right":"Pituitary gland","wrong":["Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pancreas"],"why":"ADH is secreted by the pituitary gland at the base of the brain and acts on the kidneys to save water.","hint":"The pea-sized 'master gland' just under the brain.","board":true,"hook":"Pituitary: the master gland that sends ADH to the kidneys","keys":["pituitary gland","base of the brain"]}],"2026-Q7d":[{"q":"Give the precise location and main function of the adrenal gland. (2 points)","right":"On top of each kidney; it secretes adrenaline","wrong":["In the neck; it secretes thyroxine","On top of each kidney; it secretes insulin","At the base of the brain; it secretes ADH"],"why":"An adrenal gland sits on top of each kidney; it secretes adrenaline, which prepares the body for fight or flight.","hint":"It sits on another organ like a cap; its hormone gets you ready for an emergency.","board":true,"hook":"ad-RENAL: sitting on the kidney (renal means kidney)","keys":["on top of each kidney","adrenaline"]}],"S1-Q1d":[{"q":"On the blood glucose graph, when does insulin secretion start to increase?","right":"Just after 20 minutes, as blood glucose starts to rise","wrong":["At 45 minutes, at the peak of blood glucose","At 90 minutes, when blood glucose is lowest","At 0 minutes, before the meal"],"why":"Insulin secretion rises as soon as blood glucose starts to rise after eating, just after 20 minutes, to bring the level back down.","hint":"Insulin's job is to lower blood glucose. When does the glucose start to climb?","board":"A person starts eating at 20 minutes and their blood glucose then rises to a peak at 45 minutes. When does insulin secretion start to increase?","hook":"Glucose up, insulin up: they rise together","keys":["starts to rise after eating","just after 20 minutes"]},{"q":"On the blood glucose graph, when does glucagon secretion start to increase?","right":"At about 85 minutes, when glucose falls below its resting level","wrong":["At about 25 minutes, when glucose starts to rise","At about 45 minutes, at the glucose peak","At 0 minutes, before the meal"],"why":"Glucagon is secreted when blood glucose falls back to or below its resting level, about 80 to 100 minutes, to raise it again.","hint":"Glucagon raises blood glucose. Find where the curve dips below where it started.","board":"After a meal at 20 minutes, blood glucose peaks at 45 minutes and falls below its resting level by 85 minutes. When does glucagon secretion start to increase?","hook":"Glucose down, glucagon up","keys":["below its resting level","80 to 100 minutes"]}]};
